<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alanya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Alaiye, Kalonoros</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alanya is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. The Byzantine town of Kalonoros was taken in 1221 by the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i">Kayqubad I</a>, who renamed it Alaiye and made it a winter residence and naval base, building its citadel walls, the Red Tower and a shipyard. It later passed to local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beyliks</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1471.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alanya.jpg" alt="Alanya" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ozgurmulazimoglu · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alanyafromcastle.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Alanya lies on the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, in Antalya Province, 133 km east of the city of Antalya. It occupies a small peninsula on the Gulf of Antalya, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamphylia">Pamphylia</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a> to the north and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, along about 70 km of coastline. This natural strategic position made it a local stronghold for many Mediterranean-based empires, including the Ptolemaic, Seleucid, Roman, Byzantine and Ottoman empires.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Finds in the nearby Karain Cave indicate occupation as far back as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a>, and evidence shows a port at Syedra, south of the modern city, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. The city appears in the 4th-century BCE periplus of Pseudo-Scylax, and the castle rock was probably inhabited under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s conquest it was first fortified in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>. Under Ptolemaic rule the port became a popular refuge for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> pirates. It resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> in 199 BCE but was loyal to the pirate Diodotus Tryphon, who seized the Seleucid crown from 142 to 138 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-vii-sidetes">Antiochus VII Sidetes</a> completed a new castle and port in 137 BCE.</p><p>Its names show its history. It was Korakesion in Greek, from a Luwian word for a protruding city, and Kalonoros under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. Its main political importance came in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> under the Seljuk Sultanate of Rûm and Alaeddin Kayqubad I, from whom the city takes its name; it became Alaiye. His building campaign produced Alanya Castle, dating from 1226, the Kızıl Kule (Red Tower) and the Tersane (shipyard). In the 13th and 14th centuries Italian traders called the city Candelore or Cardelloro. Mustafa Kemal Atatürk finalised the form Alanya during a visit in 1935. It became a municipality in 1872. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a> in 1923 many of its Christians were resettled in Nea Ionia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Alanya is a district and resort city of Antalya Province, with a district population of 364,180 in 2022. Tourism has been the dominant industry since 1958. On the peninsula stands Alanya Castle, a Seljuk citadel built over earlier fortifications, with the Süleymaniye mosque and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserai</a> built by the Ottoman ruler Suleiman inside it. Old walls surround much of the eastern peninsula, and within them stand historic villas, most built in the early 19th century. Akşebe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>, the castle's first commander under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i">Kayqubad I</a>, is buried in a mausoleum within the walls.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alanya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the capture of Antalya in 1207, Sinop in 1214 and <b>Alanya</b> in 1221, Seljuk Anatolia linked Mediterranean and Black Sea ports with Syria, Iraq and Iran, and Italian merchants traded in its harbours.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Alanya</b> was built up by the Seljuk sultan Kayqubad I after 1221 as a winter capital and naval base.</blockquote><a href="/state/alaiye-beylik">Alaiye Beylik</a></li></ul></section>
